interp: RETURN-FROM to one of two SAME-named nested BLOCKs resolves dynamically, not lexically.

SYMPTOM (tree-walker / interpreter)
  Two lexically-nested BLOCKs share a name; a closure defined in the OUTER block's scope
  RETURN-FROMs that name.  It should target the OUTER block (lexical), but the interpreter
  targets the INNER (dynamically nearest) one when the closure is later called from inside
  the inner block.

REPRO
  (loop for x from 42 to 43
        collect (let (o e)
                  (list 'block-odd
                        (block operation
                          (setf o (lambda (x e) (if (oddp x) (return-from operation x)
                                                    (funcall e x o))))
                          (list 'block-even
                                (block operation
                                  (setf e (lambda (x o) (if (evenp x) (return-from operation x)
                                                           (funcall o x e))))
                                  (funcall o x e)))))))
  CORRECT (lexical):  ((BLOCK-ODD (BLOCK-EVEN 42)) (BLOCK-ODD 43))
  Interpreter gives:  ((BLOCK-ODD (BLOCK-EVEN 42)) (BLOCK-ODD (BLOCK-EVEN 43)))
  For x=43, o (defined in the OUTER `operation` scope) does (return-from operation 43): it
  must exit the OUTER block -> (block-odd 43).  The interpreter instead exits the inner one.

  The byte-code VM (v1.8.22, escaping RETURN-FROM) gives the CORRECT lexical answer, because
  it resolves the block to a UNIQUE per-activation identity captured lexically by the
  closure — so the two same-named blocks are distinct.  So this is an interpreter-only bug;
  it is also a VM-vs-interpreter divergence (the VM is right).

LIKELY CAUSE
  eval.c find_block_frame(name, environment) walks the establishing environment's lexical
  frame list — but a closure's captured environment, or the block-frame's env linkage, must
  be letting the search see the inner same-named block frame that is only DYNAMICALLY (not
  lexically) in scope when o is called from within the inner block.  find_block_frame must
  match the specific block frame established in the CLOSURE'S lexical environment, not the
  nearest same-named frame reachable at call time.

FOUND-WHILE
  Implementing vm-escaping-return-from (the user's own example highlighted this exact
  same-name case).  Low priority: same-named nested escaping blocks are exotic.
